<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dou Xian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 92</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dou Xian was a Chinese general of the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> and brother of the regent Empress Dowager Dou. In 89 he led a Han army with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> allies that routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a>, and the historian Ban Gu composed a victory inscription carved at Mount Yanran in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, rediscovered in 2017. His family's dominance at court ended in 92, when Emperor He removed him and forced him to kill himself.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dou Xian was a native of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a> and belonged to the powerful Dou clan. His father, Dou Xun, fell into disgrace and died in 70, leaving him an orphan, but his fortunes changed when his two sisters entered the imperial harem in 77. The elder became Empress Zhangde, wife of Emperor Zhang of Han, the following year, and later served briefly as empress dowager and regent early in the reign of Emperor He. Dou Xian's biography is in volume 53 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-the-later-han">Hou Hanshu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 89 Dou Xian led an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> in three columns from Jilu, Manyi and Guyang, which assembled 40,000 troops at Zhuoye Mountain in the summer. His forces chased the Northern Chanyu into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a>, killing 13,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> and accepting the surrender of 200,000 from 81 tribes, while 2,000 light cavalry took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hami">Hami</a>. The campaign resulted in the destruction of the Xiongnu state.</p><p>In 90, from his camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Wuwei</a>, he sent Deputy Colonel Yan Pan against the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> defences in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>, taking Yiwu and receiving the surrender of Jushi. Although the Northern Chanyu was preparing to submit to the Han court in person, Dou Xian sent General Geng Kui and Shizi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu</a> with 8,000 light cavalry against his camp at Heyun; the Chanyu fled, leaving his family and seal behind. In 91 Geng Kui massacred 5,000 Xiongnu at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a>, and the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> moved west towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ili-river">Ili River</a> valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 92 the Dous fell in a coup. The details are unclear, but Emperor He, perhaps encouraged by his brother Prince Qing, whose mother had died at the Dous' hands, and by the eunuch Zheng Zhong, ordered the imperial guards to arrest and execute Dou Xian's associates. He sent Dou Xian and his brothers back to their marches and then ordered all but Dou Gui to commit suicide; the empress dowager kept her title but lost all power.</p><p>Ban Gu's inscription was recorded in the 5th-century Book of Later Han, and the expression to carve a stone on Yanran entered Chinese as a synonym for a decisive victory. An expedition of the Mongolian national university rediscovered it in the Inil (Delgerkhangai) Mountains of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>; the 220 legible characters of its 260 are identical to the recorded text.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dou Xian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Northern Xiongnu, who suffered famines from locust plagues, lost their chanyu Youliu to the Xianbei in 87, were crushed by the Han general <b>Dou Xian</b> at the Battle of the Altai Mountains in 89, and after another Han attack in 91 saw their chanyu flee to the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-xiongnu">Southern Xiongnu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 87 CE Xianbei warriors killed the Northern Xiongnu chanyu Youliu, and after Han armies under <b>Dou Xian</b> routed the Northern Xiongnu in 89–91 CE, the Xianbei moved west into the Mongolian steppe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xianbei">Xianbei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 89 the Han general <b>Dou Xian</b>, with Southern Xiongnu and other allies, routed the Northern Xiongnu and carved a victory inscription at Mount Yanran, found in Mongolia in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a></li></ul></section>
